    "question_text": "Use the double angle formulae to express (a) $\\sin^{2}6x$ in terms of $\\cos 12x$ and (b) $\\cos^{2}\\dfrac{x}{6}$ in terms of $\\cos\\dfrac{x}{3}$.",
    "solution_text": "(a) Use $\\cos 2A=1-2\\sin^{2}A$ with $A=6x$.\n$\\begin{aligned}\\cos 12x&=1-2\\sin^{2}6x\\\\2\\sin^{2}6x&=1-\\cos 12x\\\\\\sin^{2}6x&=\\dfrac{1-\\cos 12x}{2}\\end{aligned}$\n(b) Use $\\cos 2A=2\\cos^{2}A-1$ with $A=\\dfrac{x}{6}$.\n$\\begin{aligned}\\cos\\dfrac{x}{3}&=2\\cos^{2}\\dfrac{x}{6}-1\\\\2\\cos^{2}\\dfrac{x}{6}&=1+\\cos\\dfrac{x}{3}\\\\\\cos^{2}\\dfrac{x}{6}&=\\dfrac{1+\\cos\\frac{x}{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}$",
    "equationAlignment": {
      "mode": "continuation"
    }
  },
  {
    "question_text": "Find $\\int 4\\sin^{2}\\dfrac{3x}{4}\\,dx$.",
    "solution_text": "The double angle identity gives $\\sin^{2}\\dfrac{3x}{4}=\\dfrac{1-\\cos\\frac{3x}{2}}{2}$.\n$\\begin{aligned}&\\mathrel{\\phantom{=}}\\int 4\\sin^{2}\\dfrac{3x}{4}\\,dx\\\\&=2\\int\\left(1-\\cos\\dfrac{3x}{2}\\right)\\,dx\\\\&=2\\left(x-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\sin\\dfrac{3x}{2}\\right)+C\\\\&=2x-\\dfrac{4}{3}\\sin\\dfrac{3x}{2}+C\\end{aligned}$",
    "equationAlignment": {
      "mode": "continuation"
    }
  },
  {
    "question_text": "Find $\\int\\cos^{2}8x\\,dx$.",
    "solution_text": "Use $\\cos 16x=2\\cos^{2}8x-1$, so $\\cos^{2}8x=\\dfrac{1+\\cos 16x}{2}$.\n$\\begin{aligned}&\\mathrel{\\phantom{=}}\\int\\cos^{2}8x\\,dx\\\\&=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\int(1+\\cos 16x)\\,dx\\\\&=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\sin 16x}{16}\\right)+C\\\\&=\\dfrac{x}{2}+\\dfrac{\\sin 16x}{32}+C\\end{aligned}$",
    "equationAlignment": {
      "mode": "continuation"
    }
  },
  {
    "question_text": "Evaluate $\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}\\sin^{2}4x\\,dx$, giving an exact answer.",
    "solution_text": "Rewrite $\\sin^{2}4x=\\dfrac{1-\\cos 8x}{2}$.\n$\\begin{aligned}&\\mathrel{\\phantom{=}}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}\\sin^{2}4x\\,dx\\\\&=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}(1-\\cos 8x)\\,dx\\\\&=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left[x-\\dfrac{\\sin 8x}{8}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}\\\\&=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}-\\dfrac{\\sin\\frac{2\\pi}{3}}{8}-\\left(0-\\dfrac{\\sin 0}{8}\\right)\\right)\\\\&=\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{32}\\end{aligned}$",
    "equationAlignment": {
      "mode": "continuation"
    }
  },
  {
    "question_text": "Evaluate $\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}(4\\cos 3x)^{2}\\,dx$, giving an exact answer.",
    "solution_text": "Square the coefficient as well: $(4\\cos 3x)^{2}=16\\cos^{2}3x$. Use $\\cos^{2}3x=\\dfrac{1+\\cos 6x}{2}$.\n$\\begin{aligned}&\\mathrel{\\phantom{=}}\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}(4\\cos 3x)^{2}\\,dx\\\\&=8\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}(1+\\cos 6x)\\,dx\\\\&=8\\left[x+\\dfrac{\\sin 6x}{6}\\right]_{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}\\\\&=8\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}+\\dfrac{\\sin\\pi}{6}-\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}-\\dfrac{\\sin\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{6}\\right)\\\\&=8\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}-\\dfrac{1}{6}\\right)\\\\&=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}-\\dfrac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}$",
    "equationAlignment": {
      "mode": "continuation"
    }
  }
]
